{"markdown_body": "1. Cross-functional collaborators like Product Managers and Designers reject the keyboard-first, Markdown-only interface, creating communication silos that force the engineering team back to traditional project management tools. The early signal is a steep drop-off in non-developer workspace invites alongside tickets being manually duplicated back into legacy issue trackers.

2. The local-first state engine fails to resolve concurrent bi-directional GitHub webhook events across large engineering teams, resulting in desynchronized task states that destroy developer trust in the platform's reliability. The early signal is an influx of user bug reports citing phantom task overwrites, merge race conditions, and latency degradation in the command palette during peak deployment hours.

3. The product gets trapped as an individual developer scratchpad because power users embrace the solo triage shortcuts without engaging in collaborative team cycles, preventing bottom-up adoption from converting into paid team-wide licenses. The early signal is strong daily active usage of local command palette hotkeys paired with near-zero participation in shared sprint cycle planning and multi-seat workspace provisioning.", "node_id": "plan"}
